course: dictionary
lesson: 4588
title: Words for promises
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Henceforward» is a grand, old-fashioned word. Context clues help you work out its meaning. It appears when someone makes a promise or announces a new rule. «Henceforward I will go out seven times a day.» The speaker is starting something new and it will continue, so henceforward must mean from now on.
explain: Think of Toad at the banquet in The Wind in the Willows. He has changed, and he promises his friends he will be modest. «Henceforward» shows the change starts now and carries on into the future. The word points forward in time, from this moment onwards.
explain: In laws and old documents you may read «henceforth», which means the same thing. In everyday talk we say «from now on». Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "From now on I will go out seven times a day" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Henceforward means from now on, from this time into the future.
fact: Henceforth is a shorter word with the same meaning.
fact: Henceforward is formal and old-fashioned, so you will mostly meet it in promises, announcements and old documents.
fact: «Henceforward» is often used when someone makes a promise or starts a new rule.
fact: To check a new word, swap in "from now on" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Henceforward I will go out seven times a day.» What does henceforward mean? | Only sometimes | Before breakfast | Long ago | *From now on
exercise: An old rule says, «Henceforth, all visitors must sign in.» What does henceforth mean here? | Somewhere far away | Once upon a time | Very quietly | *From this time onwards
exercise: Ella says henceforward is a posh way of saying "from now on". Is she right? | No, it means a long time ago | *Yes, it means from now on | No, it means in the past | No, it means nothing at all
test: Which word would a formal announcement use to mean "from now on"? | Nearly | Yesterday | Sadly | *Henceforward
test: «Henceforward, we will walk to school together.» Which word in this sentence means "from now on"? | Together | *Henceforward | School | Walk
exam: Which sentence uses henceforward correctly? | The cat was henceforward asleep yesterday. | We went henceforward to the shops last week. | *From henceforward, the library closes at five. | Henceforward, I ate my lunch at noon yesterday.
